hid-fanatecff-dkms for Sparky Stereo OS
=======================================

This package builds the unchanged upstream driver (gotzl/hid-fanatecff,
revision bc2601b) with DKMS, and installs an edition udev rule:

    /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/70-sparky-hid-fanatecff.rules

It is upstream's fanatec.rules with two changes. Upstream's install.sh copies
that file to /etc/udev/rules.d/99-fanatec.rules; nothing from install.sh is run
here.

1. MODE="0666" is replaced by uaccess.
   Upstream sets mode 0666 on every device below a USB device with vendor 0eb7,
   so any user or service on the machine could write to the wheel base's raw
   nodes. The edition rule gives the same nodes (the hidraw node and the USB
   device node) to the person logged in at the seat, through an ACL (uaccess),
   and to nobody else. uaccess is applied on every action except "remove"
   (systemd v258 NEWS) and needs a file name starting with a number below 73,
   hence 70-.
2. ACTION also accepts "bind".
   The driver creates its sysfs files while it binds, after the "add" event, so
   upstream's settings could run before the files exist when a wheel is plugged
   in. (Not verified on hardware; the rule test checks the rule, not a wheel.)

What the rule opens: range, display, load, rumble, the tuning menu entries and
the LEDs are group "games", as upstream intends (their modes allow group
write). Without any rule they are root:root and only root could change the
wheel's range or tuning. Debian's "games" group is empty by default. To allow
yourself, once:

    sudo adduser "$USER" games

then log out and in. The evdev-joystick deadzone settings need the "joystick"
package (Recommends).

If you also ran upstream's install.sh, remove /etc/udev/rules.d/99-fanatec.rules:
it would put MODE="0666" back.

Test: study/packages/rigs/check-wheel-udev.py (simulated devices; fails on the
previous build, which shipped no rule).

A control the wheel's model has and that cannot be set for the games group (a
read-only /sys, a missing games group) is no longer ignored: the rule runs
/usr/lib/udev/sparky-stereo-wheel-access (package sparky-stereo-wheel-access),
which keeps the failure with sparky-stereo-problems, shown in the session with
Repair. A control the model does not have is skipped, as before.
